{
  "requirement": "A named item's `name_span` is `Some` with the span to highlight for its name\nin the original source snapshot. The highlighted text may differ from the\nname in the selector. Use `selector` to address the item. The file root and\nanonymous items have `name_span` set to `None`. `OutlineItem::new` starts with\n`None`. Language providers use `with_name_span` to set the highlight within\nthe item’s own span.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations in `requirements.sdk.outlines.name_span` are these:\n- `OutlineItem::new` \"starts with `None`\".\n- `with_name_span` is the setter that sets the highlight.\n- The `name_span` field is an optional span, `Some` or `None`.\n\nThe rest is observable behavior: which items get a span, what text it highlights, and that it lies within the item's span. A language provider's choices fall under that behavior, not under structure.\n\nBoth review roots satisfy the structural obligations:\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#impl[OutlineItem]/fn[new]` initializes `name_span: None`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#impl[OutlineItem]/fn[with_name_span]` sets `self.name_span = Some(span)`.\n\nThe field itself is declared as `pub name_span: Option<Span>` in `struct[OutlineItem]`. The root `new` constructs that type, so a reviewer reaches the declaration by following it and it needs no separate marker.\n\nI found no missing root.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement's observable obligations each map to a marked test.\n\n1. **`OutlineItem::new` starts with `None`, and `with_name_span` sets `Some`.** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` asserts `item.name_span == None` after `new`. It then calls `.with_name_span(Span::new(7,8))` and asserts `Some(Span::new(7,8))` on the cloned outline.\n\n2. **Named items get `Some` with the highlight span in the original source, and the highlighted text may differ from the selector name.**\n   - `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[rust_name_spans_highlight_spelling_in_the_original_source]` slices the original source with each span. It expects `Trait for Wrapper<T>` for the selector `impl[Wrapper<T>/Trait]`, `Wrapper<u8>`, `\"C\"` for `extern[Rust]`, and `café`.\n   - `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[language_name_spans_retain_original_bytes_when_selectors_are_normalized]` covers every non-Rust provider with a grammar: Python, TS, TSX, CSS, TOML, YAML and Markdown. It also covers plain text. It asserts the exact original-source text, for example `screen   and (color)`, `.a,  .b`, `'c/d'` and `Café   title`, where the selector is normalized. For duplicate TS functions it checks that the two spans differ.\n\n3. **The file root and anonymous items have `None`.**\n   - The language test asserts `items[0].name_span == None` for every language.\n   - Anonymous items such as the Rust `use` and the Markdown block and plain-text line are asserted `None`.\n   - `src/outline/contents.rs#mod[tests]/fn[provider_name_spans_match_selector_kind_and_item_boundaries]` checks an anonymous selector (`part@...`) with `None`, which is accepted. It checks the same selector with `Some`, which is rejected. It also rejects a named selector with `None`.\n\n4. **The name span lies within the item's own span.** The same `contents.rs` test rejects spans that start before the item (0,3) or end after it (1,4). It also rejects inverted spans (3,1) and spans that are not on a UTF-8 boundary (1,2) in the multibyte source `_é_`. It accepts the valid span (1,3).\n\nThe tests assert concrete outcomes, not just execution, and I found no unaddressed obligation or scenario.",
